# The browsers the client bundle must run in.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for three things that have to agree,
# and used to agree only by accident:
#
#   1. `vite.build.target` in nuxt.config.ts — which syntax is emitted,
#   2. `public/polyfills.js` — which runtime APIs are filled in,
#   3. `scripts/browser-baseline.mjs` — the CI check that both still hold.
#
# Why it is pinned this low: Vite 8 defaults to `baseline-widely-available`
# (Safari 16 / Chrome 107, i.e. 2022). A member on an older device then gets a
# module the browser cannot parse, the app never mounts, and the server-rendered
# markup freezes — on /register that is three loading dots that never go away,
# with no error anywhere. See docu/browser-support.md.
#
# ES2020 is the floor, not a preference: below it a module build can no longer
# express `import.meta` and dynamic `import()`, and the next step down is a
# second nomodule bundle. Browsers without ES modules at all (IE 11, Safari 10,
# Android WebView 60) cannot load this app whatever the target says.
#
# Safari 14 rather than 13.1 for one concrete reason: a hashing helper in the
# dependency tree writes the BigInt literal `32n`. BigInt arrived in Safari 14,
# and a literal is syntax — no polyfill reaches it, and rolldown says so with
# `TOLERATED_TRANSFORM: Big integer literals are not available`. That warning
# is easy to scroll past, so `scripts/browser-baseline.mjs` checks for it.
chrome >= 80
edge >= 80
firefox >= 72
safari >= 14
ios_saf >= 14
and_chr >= 80
samsung >= 13
not dead
